Create a content-addressable bundle from a directory.

This command scans the given directory, computes SHA256 checksums for each
file, and writes immutable bundle metadata into a `.bundle` directory inside
the target. The result is a self-contained, integrity-verified bundle that
can be inspected with `bundle info` and verified with `bundle verify`.

Examples:

	bundle create /path/to/files --title "My Bundle"
	bundle create /path/to/files -j           # create and print JSON summary
	bundle create /path/to/files -i SHA256SUM.txt -i '*.tmp'

Options:

- --title, -t   Set a human-friendly title for the bundle.
- --ignore, -i  Leave out matching files and directories (repeatable).
- --json, -j    Emit a machine-readable JSON summary on success.
- --verbose, -v Enable verbose logging.

Notes:

The command will create a `.bundle` directory inside the provided path to
store metadata (no files are moved). Use `bundle verify` to later check the
integrity of the bundle contents.

Ignored files stay where they are, but are not part of the bundle: they are not
in the checksum and are not pushed or imported. The patterns are stored in
.bundle/META.json, so `bundle verify` accepts these files later on.

A pattern without a slash matches a name at any depth, and one with a slash a
path from the bundle root; `/name` matches in the root only. `*` and `?` work
within one path component. Ignoring a directory ignores everything below it.
